#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-edit-a-policy.rst:36
#: 2f72cfa1fc1e4f998ced453867e48bbb
msgid "Open :program:`Qubes Policy Editor`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-edit-a-policy.rst:42
#: 11e9c5f289434f858bc12b826ebe51e4
msgid "You have to either:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-edit-a-policy.rst:44
#: 45a17c698a0d47f4a2497e98e50e316d
msgid ""
"open an existing file using :menuselection:`&File --> &Open`  or :kbd:`Ctrl` "
"+ :kbd:`O`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-edit-a-policy.rst:45
#: c21ac1de7b4f407cb27d94ffc5a6d067
msgid ""
"or create a new file using :menuselection:`&File --> &New` or :kbd:`Ctrl` + :"
"kbd:`N`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-edit-a-policy.rst:47
#: 86a6bdab26de4e79b9bf8f19644bd482
msgid ""
"In that case, you need to choose a filename. The filename can only contain "
"alphanumeric characters, underscores and hyphens. The common practice is to "
"use a name like :file:`{30}-{user}.policy` where:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-edit-a-policy.rst:49
#: 2fca997a868947e88488a11bc2817615
msgid ""
":samp:`{30}` indicates the priority (i.e. the default policies start with "
"``90`` while the policies from :program:`Qubes OS Global Config` start with "
"``50``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-edit-a-policy.rst:50
#: effa71585b4840ee8e2499e660217465
msgid ":samp:`{user}` could be any name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-edit-a-policy.rst:52
#: 6af8ea6f1615460ca8a0ccd4aac40595
msgid ""
"In order to override some clipboard policy, :file:`30-clipboard` could be a "
"good name. Starting with ``30`` makes sure that the file will be read before "
"any file starting with ``31`` or more, especially :file:`50-config-clipboard."
"policy` (this file is automatically created if you :ref:`edit-a-policy-with-"
"qubes-os-global-config`). If you want to create a policy that will never "
"override the policies from :program:`Qubes OS Global Config`, use a name "
"starting with a number between ``51`` and ``89``. The default policies from "
"Qubes OS start with ``90``, so using a number equal or superior might be "
"useless."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-edit-a-policy.rst:54
#: 250a67aed0574c8ca582274a9cbcc7de
msgid ""
"Add a line to the file. In order to prevent the *untrusted* qube from "
"pasting to the *vault* qube, the line should be:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-edit-a-policy.rst:60
#: 752f5eb22e4d4ccc9e7908892def468b
msgid ""
"If you have made any edits and if the format is correct, you will be able to "
"select :guilabel:`Save Changes` and :guilabel:`Save and Exit`, or to press :"
"kbd:`Ctrl` + :kbd:`S`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-edit-a-policy.rst:63
#: 6a04e4a50eee482f95b8bf97b646ce2a
msgid "Edit a policy with :program:`qubes-policy-editor`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-edit-a-policy.rst:65
#: 3b5845bd37594b53acf8c513346c6c87
msgid ""
":program:`qubes-policy-editor` is a command-line tool that ensures that the "
"syntax of the policy is valid. You have to run it as root:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-edit-a-policy.rst:71
#: 48aa9ddd83024cc2b45225c09ebc00de
msgid ""
"An editor will open; it will be your default editor if you have set the "
"environment variable ``$EDITOR`` or ``$VISUAL``, otherwise it will fall back "
"to :program:`vi`. After saving the file, :program:`qubes-policy-editor` will "
"check the content and tell you if there is something wrong with the syntax."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-edit-a-policy.rst:73
#: fd061630c6a344bdb63a8db651b1cf37
msgid ""
"It will open :file:`30-user.policy` by default but you can invoke the "
"program with any valid filename (only alphanumeric characters, underscores "
"and hyphens), without the ``.policy`` extension:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/how-to-edit-a-policy.rst:79
#: 0f495e9e68e24ba3a7135ca8610724f0
msgid ""
"In that case, :program:`qubes-policy-editor` will also check that the "
"filename of the policy is correct."
msgstr ""

